> So, I don't know what to do...   Assuming there is some X which is
> (Question+Questioning+Quest), how should a lojbanist term it ?

"da poi <question> zi'e poi <questioning> zi'e poi <quest>" maybe?
"lo broda no'u <question> zi'e no'u <questioning> zi'e no'u <quest>"
perhaps?

It seems like there are two parts to your question.

1) how to combine concepts in the OOP multiple inheritance kind of way.
(this one is the least problematic, IMO) lojban has a plethora of mechanisms
for combining things.  See above for two possible solutions

2) definitions for your concepts of "Question", "Questioning", and "Quest"
(all big Q)
If the concept is sufficiently complex enough that it needs a chapter in
your book to talk about you may just want to coin a fu'ivla or lujvo (make
sure it's big enough not to collide with simpler concepts) and then start
talking about what it means.  This seems to be the route that most
philosophers take.  I don't see why jbopijyskepre should change the pattern.
 Define a word that you want to contain a lot of connotation and then spend
a lifetime of writing to specify all that connotation.

